1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:06,515
Real2Simulation measures physics from real video.

2
00:00:06,915 --> 00:00:11,899
And it tells you exactly what it could not measure.

3
00:00:13,199 --> 00:00:21,338
Meta's SAM 3.1 segments and tracks the object in every frame on a Modal GPU.

4
00:00:22,638 --> 00:00:25,328
MapAnything finds the floor.

5
00:00:25,728 --> 00:00:31,292
When metric scale can't be sourced, the system abstains.

6
00:00:32,592 --> 00:00:39,910
A deterministic fitter, not a language model, fits the fall and reports its uncertainty.

7
00:00:40,310 --> 00:00:51,763
It's image-space only. The clip is probably slow motion, so this is not g in m/s².

8
00:00:53,063 --> 00:00:57,216
We seal a prediction before seeing the rest of the clip.

9
00:00:57,616 --> 00:01:06,693
Today it misses. The full tracking job ran out of GPU memory, and the ballistic fit has no floor.

10
00:01:07,093 --> 00:01:11,444
The apple lands. The prediction keeps falling.

11
00:01:12,744 --> 00:01:20,450
The system finds release and impact in the phone clip, with the scale you declare.

12
00:01:20,850 --> 00:01:29,784
Fall time: 0.433 ± 0.027 s

13
00:01:30,184 --> 00:01:38,663
Height: 0.923 ± 0.010 m

14
00:01:39,063 --> 00:01:47,070
g: 9.84 ± 1.24 m/s²

15
00:01:47,470 --> 00:01:54,228
Hero take: the track ends before impact, so lower bounds only.

16
00:01:54,628 --> 00:01:57,092
Approximate, not calibrated.

17
00:01:58,392 --> 00:02:04,767
Muse Spark interviews you about the setup, but every number it shows comes from a tool.

18
00:02:05,167 --> 00:02:14,520
Our checker flagged zero invented numbers across thirty replayed turns, on recorded scenes, not validated setups.

19
00:02:15,820 --> 00:02:20,547
The library holds simulated experiments, clearly labelled.

20
00:02:20,947 --> 00:02:27,377
The Mystery-g card is practice at Earth g.

21
00:02:27,777 --> 00:02:34,585
The lab draws a hidden g each session for a real blind test.

22
00:02:35,885 --> 00:02:39,166
Modal bill so far: under a dollar.

23
00:02:39,566 --> 00:02:45,358
Not proven: no calibrated metric source, so no physical-accuracy claim.

24
00:02:46,658 --> 00:02:52,148
Every number you saw is a pointer into the repo's benchmark.

25
00:02:52,548 --> 00:02:53,576
Try it.

